Reticulum Vector Corpus

Verified Reticulum protocol data, stored as files that can be checked
without running Python.


What this is

Each vector is a directory holding input bytes, the expected field
decomposition, and a record of where the data came from. An
implementation checks itself by decoding the input and comparing its
output to the expected decomposition.

This is not a specification. It does not define correct behaviour. It
records the observed behaviour of one implementation at one version:

    python-rns 1.4.2, commit b48b96e61676504e0a4e527b33b9a0b4495c6872,
    openssl backend

Every vector records that pin. When the pin moves, vectors are
regenerated and the differences are examined.

The backend is part of the pin because python-rns ships two curve
implementations, picks one at import time by what is installed, and
they do not agree everywhere. Vectors turn on the difference, and
tools/gen refuses to run under the other one. Which vectors is not
counted here: see doc/identity, section "Canonical signatures", and
doc/encryption, section "Contributory behaviour", which name them.

raw is what the reference produced, or configuration a human would have
typed. No line of it is a value tools/gen composed out of the
reference's own functions: the one that was, the interface key of
test/ifac, is now derived in expect from the two strings it comes from,
so cmd/dump derives it too and the two are diffed. See doc/packet.

expect is a different matter. It is tools/gen's own reading of raw, and
cmd/dump reads raw again without sharing a line of code with it. Two
readings that agree is the point; one reading copied twice would not
be.

Those two share no code and they do share a reader. Where the reading
itself is wrong, both are wrong together and the round trip agrees with
itself. CONFORMANCE is the answer to that and the only one there is:
six implementations, six languages, six authors who read the reference
without reading this. That is what makes it more than a scoreboard.


Every private key here is public

Five kinds carry a private key in every one of their vectors:
test/keyset/ and test/sign/ carry an identity key, test/encrypted/ the
recipient's, test/linkdata/ the responder's link key, and test/ifac/
the interface key, which is derived rather than given and no less
public for it. The ones named upstream are lifted from the upstream
test suite; the rest are generated from a fixed seed or derived from a
published network name and passphrase. They are published, in this
repository, on purpose, because a decryption vector cannot be checked
without one.

Never load one into anything that talks to a network. An identity built
from a key in this corpus can be impersonated by anyone who has read
this corpus, and every destination derived from it is already known.


Known limitation

Vectors generated from python-rns cannot detect bugs in python-rns.
They define "compatible" as "identical to the reference
implementation". That is the right definition for interoperability and
the wrong one for correctness.


Layout

    CONFORMANCE results of running the corpus against other
                implementations
    conformance/ the harnesses those results were produced with
    doc/        byte layouts and derivation rules; start at doc/fields,
                and at doc/harness to check your own implementation
    test/       the vectors
    cmd/        dump, a decoder written in C; check, a shell script
    tools/      gen, the Python generator

What a consumer needs is test/, doc/ and cmd/. The other two are the
larger half of the repository and neither is part of the corpus:
tools/ needs Python and the pinned checkout, and conformance/ holds six
harnesses in six languages, needing Go, Rust, Node, Elixir and a C++
compiler between them. They are here because a result is only evidence
if the thing that produced it is on file. Nothing under either is
needed to check an implementation against these vectors.

doc/fields is the index of every name an expect file can carry, what it
holds and which kinds carry it. It is the shortest path from this
README to a working harness. The vectors are the authority on which
names exist; the document is where what they mean is written.

dump needs a C compiler and nothing else. SHA-256, HMAC-SHA256, HKDF
and AES-256-CBC decryption are written out in cmd/; tweetnacl supplies
Ed25519 signing and verification, SHA-512 and X25519 and is vendored
unmodified, see cmd/VENDOR.

gen needs Python and a checkout of the pinned reference implementation.
It looks for one in ../upstream and takes --checkout PATH otherwise.
Consumers of the corpus need neither.


Checking an implementation

    make
    cmd/check

runs the corpus against cmd/dump. To check your own implementation,
make it print the same field format and substitute it for dump:

    DUMP=./my-implementation cmd/check

A decoder that implements only the decode direction can skip the
round trip described below:

    DUMP=./my-implementation ENCODE=no cmd/check

It gives up most of what the corpus can tell it. Decoding proves an
implementation reads what python-rns writes; the round trip is the only
thing here that says anything about what it writes.

A decoder that covers some kinds and not others exits 77 for the rest.
Those vectors are reported as skipped and named, and are not counted
against it:

    40 passed, 9 failed, corpus 610068843, 1 skipped: ifac

doc/harness is the whole of what a program has to do to be substituted
for dump here, and cmd/dump -l lists the kinds it can be asked for. It
also shows how to run this from an implementation's own CI, which is
the two commands above and nothing to install.

The corpus number is a checksum over test/INDEX and every raw and
expect. It says what the result was measured against. A count alone
does not: two vectors have changed shape without changing the count,
and any number recorded elsewhere against the old shape would still
have looked current. Record it beside any result kept outside this
repository.

    make verify

additionally regenerates every vector from the pinned reference
implementation and fails if a single byte would change, and checks the
vendored file against the hashes in cmd/VENDOR.

doc/ and the programs name the reference by symbol:

    RNS/Packet.py#get_hashable_part

The anchor is the name to grep for. There is no line number, so there
is nothing in a citation that goes stale while the pin holds; when the
pin moves and a symbol is gone, the vectors are regenerated anyway.

The regeneration is the evidence behind the source line in every meta
file. It needs Python and the checkout; cmd/check alone needs neither.


One vector

Every vector is three files, and the smallest kind shows the shape.
test/identity/upstream-0 is a public key and what is derived from it:

    $ cmd/dump identity test/identity/upstream-0/raw
    public_key         4219d9ce29da42bc823b66b24a051c65...c616501b
    x25519_public      4219d9ce29da42bc823b66b24a051c65...62924930
    ed25519_public     1cd07b674027511e73469c4edec93634...c616501b
    identity_hash      650b5d76b6bec0390d1f8cfca5bd33f9

That output is test/identity/upstream-0/expect, byte for byte, and that
is the whole contract. A decoder passes a vector when

    my-decoder <kind> test/<kind>/<name>/raw

prints the expect file. Where the determinism class is encode, cmd/check
also runs

    my-decoder -e <kind> test/<kind>/<name>/expect

and requires raw back.

The hex above is shortened to fit this page. Nothing in the format wraps
or elides, so read a vector rather than a description of one:

    cat test/pathrequest/tagged/*

That one is a whole packet and uses no cryptography. Its expect file
holds the eleven header fields every packet kind carries, then five of
its own. doc/fields is the index of all of them.


Vector format

    test/INDEX          every vector in the corpus, one per line

    test/<kind>/<name>/

        meta      provenance and determinism class
        raw       input, hex, one blob per line
        expect    field decomposition, one field per line

Vectors are named after what they record, not numbered. Inserting a
case does not renumber the rest, and doc/ can refer to one by name.

cmd/check refuses to run against a test/ that does not hold exactly
what INDEX lists, and names what is missing. Without that, a corpus
with forty vectors removed reports "8 passed, 0 failed" and exits
zero, and a count is not a claim about anything.

meta records these fields, spelled in lower case as shown:

    source        which implementation and version produced this
    determinism   decode or encode
    purpose       one line

cmd/check requires all three and rejects an unknown determinism class.
A field nothing reads is a field that drifts.

The kind is not among them. It is the directory the vector is filed
under, and recording it twice only bought a rule to keep the two
agreeing.

A vector of the encode class carries in expect everything raw contains,
so raw can be rebuilt from it; cmd/check does exactly that and diffs
the result against raw. Nearly every vector is of that class. Those
that are not name their reason:

    test/signature/, test/sign/   expect records the 950-byte message
                                  by length and digest, not by content
    the rejections                the bytes that broke the rule are not
                                  printed, so expect names the rule and
                                  the numbers behind it instead

A broken rule does not by itself put a vector in the decode class.
short-plaintext is broken inside a plaintext the packet around it
decoded whole, so expect holds every byte of raw; test/linkdata holds
four of those. See doc/fields, section Rejection.

The class says whether expect is total, not whether the reference
could produce these bytes a second time. Most Reticulum objects
contain randomness and cannot be produced twice; expect records what
that randomness came out as, and raw follows from it.

An object whose input is not the object itself carries that input in
expect too, so that expect stays total: the recipient key of an
encrypted packet, the link request a proof answers. Every such value is
already a line of raw. See doc/.

Every value in expect is one of three things: hex, a decimal number, or
a keyword from a fixed set. Byte strings are always hex, names
included. A destination name is arbitrary bytes, and printed as text a
newline in an aspect would end the line early and let a vector forge or
hide a field.

No value is ever empty. Hex cannot spell the empty byte string, and a
field name followed by nothing is a line with a trailing space that
cannot be told from a truncated one, so the empty byte string is
written "-" like every other absent value. test/destination/empty-aspect
and test/plain/empty are where it happens.

The number of hex lines in raw depends on the kind and is documented
in doc/.

An object that cannot be decoded yields the rule it broke and the
numbers behind it, rather than a single word:

    invalid            short-payload
    payload_length     147
    minimum_length     148


Relationship to the upstream test suite

The upstream suite is a regression suite. It builds objects with RNS
and validates them with RNS, and asserts nothing about the resulting
bytes. It cannot detect an incompatibility in a different
implementation.

Where upstream does provide reusable values, they are adopted here
verbatim and their origin recorded in meta, rather than re-derived.

Primitives that every language already provides are not tested here.


Upstream license

Reticulum is licensed permissively with two added restrictions: no use
in systems intended to harm humans, and no use in AI or language model
training datasets. Adopted values are attributed in the meta file of
the vector that carries them.
